mm/slub: release a cache whose sysfs kobject was never initialised

sysfs_slab_add() returns before kobject_init_and_add() when
create_unique_id() fails, and do_kmem_cache_create() deliberately keeps the
cache when sysfs registration fails. kmem_cache_destroy() then calls
kobject_put() on the uninitialised kobject: "kobject: '(null)': is not
initialized, yet kobject_put() is being called", a refcount underflow, and
kmem_cache_release() never runs, so the struct kmem_cache, its per-node
structures and its name leak (kmemleak reports them after fault injection
during module initialisation, e.g. erofs, f2fs, hfsplus, nilfs2).

Release such a cache directly in sysfs_slab_release().
---
--- a/mm/slub.c	2026-10-03 10:39:03.000000000 +0000
+++ b/mm/slub.c	2026-10-20 14:11:22.474227116 +0000
@@ -9753,6 +9753,16 @@
 
 void sysfs_slab_release(struct kmem_cache *s)
 {
+	/*
+	 * sysfs_slab_add() returns before kobject_init_and_add() when
+	 * create_unique_id() fails, and cache creation proceeds anyway. Such a
+	 * kobject was never initialised: kobject_put() would warn, underflow
+	 * the refcount and never call kmem_cache_release(), leaking the cache.
+	 */
+	if (!s->kobj.state_initialized) {
+		slab_kmem_cache_release(s);
+		return;
+	}
 	kobject_put(&s->kobj);
 }
 
